    <statement><p>Convert the following point from rectangular to cylindrical coordinates:
<me>(5,-5\sqrt{3},1).</me></p>
<p><m>(r,\theta,z) =</m> <fillin characters="20" name="AnSwEr0001"/></p>
<p><alert> Usage: </alert>To enter a point, for example <m>(x,y,z)</m>, type "(x, y, z)".</p>
</statement>
    <answer>
      <p>
        <m>\left(10,\frac{5\pi }{3},1\right)</m>
      </p>
    </answer>
    <solution><m>(x,y,z)=(5,-5\sqrt{3},1)</m>. We first find <m>r</m>:
<me>r=\sqrt{x^2+y^2}=\sqrt{(5)^2+(-5\sqrt{3})^2}=10.</me>
Since the point <m>(x,y)=(5,-5\sqrt{3})</m> lies in the fourth quadrant, <m>\frac{3\pi}{2}\leq \theta\leq 2\pi</m>. Hence
<me>\tan \theta=\frac{y}{x}=\frac{-5\sqrt{3}}{5}=-\sqrt{3},</me>
so <m>\theta=\frac{5 \pi}{3}</m>.



The cylindrical coordinates are thus
<me>(r,\theta,z)=\left( 10, \frac{5 \pi}{3}, 1 \right).</me>


</solution>
